# Introduction

This tutorial provides a hands-on introduction to R and RStudio. It complements
the material on [StartR](https://startr.numbat.space/), acting both as revision
and extension of that content.

The structure of this tutorial is typical of what you will do over the
semester. You will need to think about how to manipulate a given dataset, how
to visualise it effectively, and what you can learn from it. To start with, we
focus mostly on developing your knowledge of R and programming skills. As the
semester progresses, we will shift emphasis to learn and practice statistical
concepts and skills.

**Learning Goals for Week 1**

-   Use **R** and **RStudio** on your own device.
-   Learn to install and load **R packages**.
-   Learn about **Quarto** files and reproducible research.
-   Learn about the **tidyverse**.
-   Learn some basic R commands to manipulate and plot data.
-   Compose your own answers to questions and render a document for submission.
-   Learn how to diagnose errors.

If you are already a confident user of R and RStudio, this introduction will be
straightforward. You will finish quickly and can use your time to expand your
capabilities through the later StartR modules, or investigate other resources.
You can also use this introduction as a reference later in the unit.

If you are an R beginner, or need a refresher, take you time to work through
each part of this tutorial. Perhaps make a list of the different packages or
commands as you learn them, and note on which tutorial or other reference on
which they appear so that you can find them again when you need to do something
similar later on.

For everyone, as the unit progresses, you are encouraged to explore beyond what
the tasks dictate; a willingness to experiment will make you a much better
programmer and data analyst. It will also give you the space to be able to
think beyond the code, and to appreciate that having a computational
perspective can really help you to *understand* data and the relevance (and
limitations!) of modern statistics/econometrics/actuarial/analytics techniques.

Once you have both R and RStudio installed, you are ready to start the tutorial
activities. So let's begin with the fundamental building blocks of R and
RStudio: the interface, reading in data, and some basic commands.

