# Launch RStudio and create a Project

Go ahead and launch RStudio.

## Project

A project in RStudio is essentially a folder that contains all the relevant
files in one place. With the help of the project, you don't need to worry about
whether the file you want to open is in the current working directory (the
place you are working at).

Create a project for this unit. Go to **File** and then **New Project**. Select
**New Directory** if you want to create a new folder to contain you files, or
select **Existing Directory** if you want to use an existing folder.

Be sure to create a data folder; call it `data`, to stick with convention. It
is up to you how you set up the directories. You could create a folder for each
week; if so, then also create a `data` folder in each one.

Remember to open this project every time you want to work on this unit. We
recommend **not** saving the "Workspace image" (the data you have loaded, the
functions you have defined, etc.) when you quit R or switch to a different
project, so you can have a fresh start next time you open RStudio.

## Packages

We will use the `tidyverse` and `tinytex` packages (you should have seen these
in the StartR module), and we will always use them. It is probably the first
thing that you should add to your Quarto file -- more on this below.

### The tinytex package

The `tinytex` package enables us to generate publication quality documents
directly to PDF. It also allows us to use LaTeX to create equations in our
documents. The LaTeX commands also work if we render to HTML, but we need an
extra step to convert this to PDF. Rendering directly to PDF is our strong
preference in this unit.

The `tinytex` package can be somewhat temperamental (and takes a while to
install). If you have any issues, please attend a consultation and we will try
and help you. Please do not ask for help with tinytex in the tutorial, we will
not have enough time to diagnose the problem. Simply render to HTML.

### The tidyverse package

The `tidyverse` package is actually a suite of packages, including those that
are designed for data wrangling and data visualisation. These packages share
common philosophies and are designed to work together. Over time the packages
contained within the tidyverse have changed. You can find more about the
packages in the tidyverse at <https://www.tidyverse.org/>.

Throughout this unit, we will use many of the packages in the tidyverse. For
today, the key ones are:

-   `readr`: Reads data in from various types of file and makes it available in
    R.  
-   `dplyr`: Manipulates data (e.g. transform, filter, select, summarise)
-   `tidyr`: Tools for putting data into a "tidy" format that is suitable for
    analysis and plotting.
-   `ggplot2`: A suite of tools for plotting data.

The other packages in the tidyverse are more specialised, but we will use
several of them occasionally, including:

-   `purrr`: Primarily useful when you need to do apply the same function to
    every element of a list.
-   `stringr`: For manipulating strings (sets of characters).
-   `forcats`: A toolbox for manipulating categorical variables.

## Install the packages

Throughout the unit we will need more packages. Sometimes this will just be to
use a specialised function. So we need to learn how to install a new package.

If tidyverse is not already available in your R environment, install it by
typing the following line of code into the *Console* pane of your RStudio
session. (You do not need to install the package again if it is already
installed.)

```{r}
#| eval: false
install.packages("tidyverse")
```

Note: You may need to select a server from which to download; any of the
servers should work.

You can find the list of installed package names listed in the RStudio
*Packages* pane. Notice a description of the package and the installed version
number is also listed.

*An R tip:* In the code above you'll notice that there is an opening bracket
and a closing bracket. Similarly, the quotation marks (`"`) come in pairs.
RStudio tries to help you out here by colouring anything inside the quotation
marks a different colour. And if you put your cursor near a bracket, its pair
should be highlighted. This will be useful later when we want to do more than
one thing at a time.

## Load package libraries

Next, you need to load the `tidyverse` package into your current session (you
shouldn't need to load the `tinytex` package, but you can if you wish). We do
this with the `library()` function. Run the following lines in your *Console*
pane:

```{r}
#| message: false
library(tidyverse)
```

You always need to load a package before you can use any of the functions in
it. This has to be done every time you start a new RStudio session. If you
forget to do this, R will complain because it won't be able to find the
function you're looking for.

(R complains a lot. This might be discouraging at first, but don't take it
personally. It's only a computer system so it is not smart enough to work out
everything on its own. Treat the warning and errors messages as reminders about
steps that need to be done.)

Usually a package will only need to be installed on to your machine once, but
you need to load them each time you start a new RStudio session. We typically
use the console to install packages, and load packages in a code chunk at the
beginning of our file (see "[Load Arbuthnot's dataset]" below).

