# Dr. Arbuthnot’s baptism records

John Arbuthnot was a Scottish physician. In 1710 he published a paper[^1] that
examined birth records in London for each of the 82 years from 1629 to 1710.
Actually the data he used were counts of baptisms, by year and gender, but
these are used as a proxy for the number of births.

[^1]: Arbuthnot, J. (1710). “An argument for divine providence, taken from the
    constant regularity observed in the births of both sexes,”
    \textit{Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society}, Vol 27, pp
    186–190.

The data from Arbuthnot’s study is contained in the file `arbuthnot.csv`,
provided with these notes. It consists of three columns, named `year`, `boys`
and `girls`. The `boys` and `girls` columns show the number of each that were
born in London for each year as specified in the `year` column.

## Load Arbuthnot's dataset

Save the file `arbuthnot.csv` to your project and put it in a folder called
`data`.

In your Quarto file, create a new code chunk and add the following code:

```{r}
#| label: load-packages
#| eval: true
#| echo: true
#| message: false
library(tidyverse)

arbuthnot <- read_csv("data/arbuthnot.csv")
```

### A note about code chunks

Code chunks are where Quarto knows to use R and put the results into our
report. Notice that the chunk is shaded, this reminds us that this section is R
code.

In Quarto, code chunks are created by typing 3 backticks (\`\`\`) followed by
"`r`" in curly brackets (no space), then 3 more backticks at the end of the
chunk.

In the curly brackets, we can also add add a name and set various options. The
chunk above is named `load-packages` (see this in the Quarto version of the
notes). The options control how the code will be run and displayed when the
document is rendered. Some of they key options are:

-   `eval = TRUE` or `eval = FALSE`: Whether to run the code (the default) or
    not
-   `echo = TRUE` or `echo = FALSE`: Whether to print the code (the default) or
    not
-   `message = TRUE` or `message = FALSE`: Whether or not to print diagnostic
    messages generated by running the code.
-   `error = TRUE` or `error = FALSE`: This specifies what to do if any errors
    occur. The default behaviour is `error = FALSE`, which stops the
    compilation every time you see an error. If you know the code has an error
    and you want the document to continue rendering anyway (for example, the
    purpose of the chunk is to demonstrate a particular error), you can set
    `error = TRUE`.

We can put any number of these options (and several others) together, separated
by commas.

The settings for the chunk above will mean that during rendering it will:

-   run the code chunk,
-   print the code chunk in the output, and
-   **not** print any diagnostic messages from R.

The code itself loads the `tidyverse` package, then creates a tibble called
`arbuthnot` by reading in the CSV file. If all has gone well, you should see an
entry called `arbuthnot` in your Environment tab in the top-right pane in
RStudio, with 82 observations of 3 variables.

