## Tibbles

The `arbuthnot` object you have created is called a *tibble*. This is one of
R's most important data types and is used widely throughout the tidyverse. It
holds *rectangular* or *tabular* data. That is, data where each row has the
same number of entries, so it forms a "rectangle" with a particular number of
rows and columns. Each column can be different types (numbers, strings, dates,
etc.)

Tibbles are a relatively recent addition to R and comec from the `tibble`
package, which is part of the tidyverse. For information, see [Chapter 10, in R
for Data Science](https://r4ds.had.co.nz/tibbles.html).

*R tip*: Names are case-sensitive in R. That means you can refer to the above
tibble by `arbuthnot`, but typing `Arbuthnot` will not work.

## The Viewer pane

View the `arbuthnot` tibble by clicking on it in the *Environment* pane. This
will load into a *Viewer* pane in the upper-left part of RStudio, next to the
*Editor* pane(s).

The `arbuthnot` tibble contains 82 rows and 3 columns. While you will be able
to see four columns of numbers in the *Viewer* pane, the first column is not
actually part of the tibble. Each row represents a different year, shown in the
first column of the tibble, and the second and third columns contain the
numbers of boys and girls (respectively) baptised in the year corresponding to
the entry in column 1. Use the scrollbar on the right-hand side of the *Viewer*
pane to examine the complete data set.

The format of the tibble is important. Each of the column entries in a given
row relates to the same year, so the 82 rows correspond to 82 distinct *cases*.
The three columns represent three different attributes of a case, or
*variables*, namely the *year*, the number of *boys* baptised and the number of
*girls* baptised.

You can close the data viewer by clicking on the "$\times$" in the Viewer tab.

## Take a 'glimpse' at the data

Another way to take a look at this tibble is to use the `glimpse()` function:

```{r}
#| eval: false
glimpse(arbuthnot)
```

This function comes from the `broom` package, which is included in the
tidyverse. To try it out, just type the command above into your *Console* pane.

The `glimpse()` function does not change the format of the tibble, it merely
uses the tibble as an input, and prints out an alternative format without
modifying the tibble itself.

You could also include the above line of code in your file, inside a code
chunk. Make sure it comes *after* the command that loads the dataset (you have
to load the data before you can do something with it!).

## Print a variable

We can access a variable contained in a single column of a tibble using command
such as:

```{r}
arbuthnot$boys
```

This command will only show the number of boys baptised each year. The dollar
sign (`$`) extracts the column of the tibble according to the name that follows
it, in this case it takes the column `boys` from the tibble `arbuthnot`.

*RStudio tip:* When you are typing, you can often just type the first few
characters and then press the tab key on your keyboard to complete the typing
for you. When there are multiple options, they will pop up for you to see. Try
typing `arbuthnot$` and then press the tab key. You should see the three column
names come up as options.

Notice the way R has printed these data. When we looked at the complete tibble,
we saw 82 rows, one on each line of the display. Now having extracted only a
single column, the data are no longer structured in a table with other
variables, and instead are displayed one after another. This object is an
example of a *vector*, which is used to represent a set of numbers of the same
type. R has added numbers in square brackets along the left-hand side of the
printout to indicate locations within the vector. For example, 5218 follows
`[1]`, indicating that 5218 is the first entry in the vector. And if \[33\]
starts a line, then that would mean the first number on that line would
represent the 33rd entry in the vector.

