## Data visualisation

R has some powerful functions for making graphics easily. We can create a
simple plot of the number of girls baptised per year with the command:

```{r}
#| fig-width: 5
#| fig-height: 4
ggplot(data = arbuthnot, aes(x = year, y = girls)) +
  geom_point()
```

We use the `ggplot()` function to build plots as follows:

-   The first argument is always the dataset.

-   Next, you specify how the variables from the dataset should be assigned to
    visual dimensions ("aesthetics") of the plot, such as the x- and y-axes.
    You do this with the command: `aes(x = x_variable, y = y_variable)`.

-   Finally, you specify geometric objects ("geoms") to add to the plot.
    Somewhat unconventionally, these are added using the plus symbol (`+`). In
    this case, we want to create a scatter plot, so we draw points by using
    `geom_point()`. If you want a line plot instead, you would use
    `geom_line()`.

We can customise the visualisation further in many different ways. For example,
to draw pink triangles instead of black circles, we would use:
`geom_point(colour = "pink", shape = 17)`.

To find out more, a good reference is the ggplot2 cheat sheet:
<https://rstudio.github.io/cheatsheets/html/data-visualization.html>

R also documents all of its functions extensively. To learn what a function
does and its arguments that are available to you, just type in a question mark
followed by the name of the function that you’re interested in. Try the
following in your *Console*:

```{r}
#| eval: false
#| echo: true
?ggplot
```

Notice that the help file appears in the lower-right panel. You can toggle
between plots and help files using the tabs at the top of that panel.

Finally, if you ever get stuck or get an error message that is confusing, you
can usually find help online. Type the error message into Google and it will
very often point you to the answer.

