## R as a big calculator

Now, suppose we want to plot the total number of baptisms over time. To compute
the total, we could use the fact that R is really just a big calculator. We can
type in mathematical expressions like:

```{r}
#| eval: false
#| echo: true
5218 + 4683
```

to get the total number of baptisms in 1629. We could repeat this once for each
year, but there is a faster way. If we add the *vector* of baptisms for boys to
that of girls, R will compute *all* sums with only a single command.

```{r}
#| eval: false
#| echo: true
arbuthnot$boys + arbuthnot$girls
```

When you run this command, you will see it prints a vector containing 82
numbers, each one representing the sum we’re after for each year in the data
set. (Take a look at a few of them and verify that they are right.)

Unfortunately, the command above doesn't save the results. We could define a
new object, using the command:

```{r}
#| eval: false
#| echo: true
total <- arbuthnot$boys + arbuthnot$girls
```

However, the vector named `total` created in the above code chunk would be
completely separate from the original `arbuthnot` tibble. It would be more
convenient to include the constructed `total` variable as another column in the
tibble.

## Adding new variables to a tibble

Since we want to use this new `total` vector to generate some plots, we’ll want
to save it as a new column in the `arbuthnot` tibble. We do this using the
`mutate()` command from the `dplyr` package (loaded as part of the tidyverse).
This is a useful function that will add new columns to a tibble that are
constructed from the old columns.

We can do it as follows:

```{r}
#| eval: true
#| echo: true
arbuthnot <- mutate(arbuthnot, total = boys + girls)
```

It is possible to add several new columns at once. We just specify each one in
turn, as a separate argument. For instance, if we also wanted a sex ratio
(girls/boys), we could get that with:

```{r}
#| eval: true
#| echo: true
arbuthnot <- mutate(arbuthnot, total = boys + girls, ratio = girls / boys)
```

If we also wanted the proportion of girls, we could add that too:

```{r}
#| eval: true
#| echo: true
arbuthnot <- mutate(arbuthnot,
                    total      = boys + girls,
                    ratio      = girls / boys,
                    prop_girls = girls / total)
```

Notice that we used `total` as part of our definition of `prop_girls`. This
works fine because `mutate()` evaluates the arguments in turn from left to
right, which means the `total` column exists by the time we get to
`prop_girls`. In contrast, the following version would generate an error
because `total` doesn't exist:

```{r}
#| eval: true
#| echo: true
#| error: true
arbuthnot <- mutate(arbuthnot,
                    prop_girls = girls / total,
                    total      = boys + girls, 
                    ratio      = girls / boys)
```

In addition to simple mathematical operators like subtraction and division, you
can ask R to make comparisons like greater than (`>`), less than (`<`), and
equality (`==`). For example, we can ask if the number of births of boys
outnumber that of girls in each year with the expression:

```{r}
#| eval: true
#| echo: true
arbuthnot <- mutate(arbuthnot, more_boys = boys > girls)
```

This command adds a new variable to the `arbuthnot` tibble containing the
values `TRUE` or `FALSE` depending on whether the respective year had more boys
than girls. This variable contains a different kind of data than we have
encountered so far. All other columns in the `arbuthnot` tibble have values
that are numerical (the year, and the number of boys and girls). Here, we have
now asked R to create logical variables (also known as Boolean variables),
which only have the values `TRUE` or `FALSE`. In general, data analysis will
involve many different kinds of data types. One reason for using R is that it
is easily able to represent and compute with many of them.

