# Better R coding

## Coding in style

In some of the previous code chunks, we wrapped the code over several lines and
indented the code to make it more readable. This is good practice, to help
others (and your future self) easily understand your code.

There are a few things to remember when doing wrapping code:

-   You can only wrap lines that are inside brackets. Otherwise R doesn't know
    that there's more coming.
-   You should end every line with an indication that some more code is coming.
    Here we have a comma (`,`) at the end of each line, but a `+` or anything
    that requires something to the right of it will work.

Some other points of style to notice:

-   All names are in lower case
-   Words in the same name are separated by underscores (`_`)
-   There are spaces on either side of operators (`+`, `/`, `<-`)
-   There is one space to the right of each comma
-   There is no space near brackets.

This might seem super pedantic and annoying, but it's one of the most important
but least flashy coding skills. Keeping consistent style that is easy to read
is a core professional standard. The tidyverse style guide
(<https://style.tidyverse.org>) is a handy reference that we recommend you
follow when writing your R code.

## Simplicity via piping

An important element of modern R code is the %\>% operator, which is called the
*pipe operator*. Pretty much all of the commands above should be piped, to
improve readability.

If you've never seen it before, it can seem like a bit of an odd beast. It does
one simple thing: it takes the output of the expression on the left and inserts
("pipes") it into the first argument of the function that follows it.

In the language of mathematical functions, $x$ %\>% $f(y)$ is equivalent to
$f(x, y)$.

For example, we could write that first mutate statement in the previous section
as follows:

```{r}
#| eval: false
arbuthnot <- arbuthnot %>% mutate(total = boys + girls)
```

Here's how to interpret what the code is doing:

“Take the `arbuthnot` dataset and pipe it into the `mutate()` function. Mutate
the `arbuthnot` tibble by creating a new variable, called `total`, that is the
sum of the variables called `boys` and `girls`. Then assign the resulting
tibble to the object called `arbuthnot`. In other words, overwrite the old
`arbuthnot` tibble with the new version that contains the new variable.”

This is equivalent to going through each row and adding up the boys and girls
counts for that year and recording that value in a new column called `total`.

The assignment operator (`<-`) takes the output of everything to the right of
it and saves it into the object on its left. In this case, since we already
have an object called `arbuthnot`, this command updates (overwrites) it with
the version we obtain after running `mutate()`.

What makes the piping approach so powerful is that you can chain several pipes
together.

For instance the following commands give the same output:

```{r}
#| eval: false
# Version without piping.
arbuthnot_mutated <- mutate(arbuthnot, total = boys + girls, ratio = girls / boys)

# Version using piping.
arbuthnot_mutated <- arbuthnot %>% 
  mutate(total = boys + girls) %>%
  mutate(ratio = girls / boys)
```

The code using pipes is easier to read because each line is doing a single
step. We can read it as follows:

"Take `arbuthnot`, add a `total` column and then add a `ratio` column."

## Commenting out code

The code chunk above shows how to add comments to code using `#`. This allows
you to add any text to your code and R won't try to run it.

One handy way to use this is to "turn off" any code that you no longer wish to
run, but would like to retain for later. This is called "commenting out" the
code.

